findings.body:
Password-reset tokens must be unguessable. This replaces `randomBytes(32)` (256-bit CSPRNG, 64 hex chars) with `Math.floor(Math.random() * 1e12).toString(36)`, which is (1) not cryptographically secure and (2) at most ~10¹² values, rendered as an 8–9 character base-36 string — trivially brute-forceable if an attacker can attempt resets or hit a token-check endpoint.

For edge runtimes, use Web Crypto instead of Node `crypto.randomBytes`:

```suggestion
  const bytes = new Uint8Array(32);
  globalThis.crypto.getRandomValues(bytes);
  return Array.from(bytes, (b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, "0")).join("");
```

`getRandomValues` is the edge-standard CSPRNG (Workers, Vercel Edge, Deno, modern Node) and keeps the 32-byte token strength.
